1. `option_bag_junk_drawer` — `describeCheckout`

The single `CheckoutOptions` bag mixes checkout identity, market context, and retry policy:

```ts
interface CheckoutIdentity {
  plan: string;
  role: string;
  status: string;
}

interface MarketContext {
  region: string;
  currency: string;
}

interface RetryPolicy {
  shouldRetry: boolean;
}

function describeCheckout(
  checkout: CheckoutIdentity,
  market: MarketContext,
  retry: RetryPolicy,
): string {
  return [
    checkout.plan,
    checkout.role,
    market.region,
    market.currency,
    checkout.status,
    String(retry.shouldRetry),
  ].join(":");
}
```

Today, an agent may treat `options` as an extensible junk drawer, add unrelated fields, make required values optional, or misunderstand which fields belong together. Narrow records expose those ownership boundaries and make call-site omissions type errors.

2. `return_shape_roulette` — `resolvePaymentResult`

Give the three outcomes an explicit discriminant and return type:

```ts
type PaymentResult =
  | {
      kind: "error";
      code?: string;
      message?: string;
    }
  | {
      kind: "redirect";
      url?: string;
      permanent: boolean;
    }
  | {
      kind: "success";
      value?: string;
      cached: boolean;
    };

function resolvePaymentResult(input: PaymentInput): PaymentResult {
  if (input.error) {
    return { kind: "error", code: input.code, message: input.message };
  }

  if (input.redirect) {
    return { kind: "redirect", url: input.url, permanent: false };
  }

  return { kind: "success", value: input.value, cached: false };
}
```

Currently the three shapes have as little as 0% key overlap, while only two contain `ok`. An agent could assume `result.ok` always exists, read `value` from a redirect, or add a fourth undocumented shape. The discriminated union makes callers narrow by `kind` and enables exhaustive handling.

3. `negative_flag_maze` — `canRunBilling`

Invert both flags at the boundary and express the predicate directly:

```ts
export function canRunBilling(
  isBillingEnabled: boolean,
  shouldRetry: boolean,
): boolean {
  return isBillingEnabled && shouldRetry;
}
```

Preferably, use a named parameter object if these arguments can be confused at call sites:

```ts
interface BillingRunPolicy {
  isBillingEnabled: boolean;
  shouldRetry: boolean;
}

export function canRunBilling(policy: BillingRunPolicy): boolean {
  return policy.isBillingEnabled && policy.shouldRetry;
}
```

The current `!disableBilling && !skipRetry` requires two mental inversions. An agent adding another condition could invert only one flag, accidentally change `&&` to `||`, or pass the two positional booleans in the wrong order. Positive names also resolve the overlapping `boolean_naming_drift` finding.
